import java.io.*;

// FileReader can throw FileNotFoundException (checked) -> must declare or catch
String readFirstLine(String path) throws IOException { // declared with throws
    try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(path))) {
        return br.readLine();
    }
    // Without the throws (or a try-catch) this would NOT compile.
}
```

Checked exceptions (`IOException`, `SQLException`) model recoverable external failures, so the compiler makes the caller acknowledge them.

```java
// NumberFormatException extends RuntimeException (unchecked)
int parseAge(String raw) {
    return Integer.parseInt(raw); // may throw at runtime; no throws needed
}

int age = parseAge("oops"); // compiles fine, blows up at runtime -> it's a bug to fix
```

Unchecked exceptions (`NullPointerException`, `IllegalArgumentException`) signal programming errors, so Java does not force handling — you fix the cause instead.

// Old approach: manual finally block
public String readFileLegacy(String path) throws IOException {
    BufferedReader reader = null;
    try {
        reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(path));
        return reader.readLine();
    } catch (IOException e) {
        System.err.println("Error reading file");
        throw e;
    } finally {
        if (reader != null) {
            reader.close(); // always called, even if return was executed
        }
    }
}

// Modern approach: try-with-resources (Java 7+)
public String readFileModern(String path) throws IOException {
    try (var reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(path))) {
        return reader.readLine(); // reader.close() called automatically
    } catch (IOException e) {
        System.err.println("Error reading file");
        throw e;
    }
    // No finally needed! Try-with-resources handles cleanup
}
